The source dependency graph is now published (`12f7ef5`). It has 224 nodes and 567 edges, and every completion gate passes:
- every node reviewed by someone other than its extractor;
- full source coverage, everything reachable, all four anchors correctly bound;
- an independent global review.

Its only defect, four missing convergence edges, was fixed by a separate writer and rechecked.

The Lean work has run ahead of the graph and matches it. Still running: `lt-sol17` (slicing, B8), `lt-sol10` (energy deficit, B11) and `lt-sol12` (currying and Pauli bound); `lt-sol13` is waiting on `lt-sol12`.

The roadmap worker `lt-x0` (GPT-6.1-Sol, high) is running. It will:
- download the open sources (Lieb–Lebowitz is in the Advances in Mathematics open archive; Lieb–Simon 1977 is already here);
- write `[private reference redacted]extensions-roadmap.md`.

For each extension, the roadmap will give:
- precise target theorems in terms of our existing definitions, plus every new definition each one needs: ground-state energy, states confined to a domain, a weak eigenfunction for the ionization bound, the Thomas–Fermi functional;
- the proof route and its hard parts;
- what the existing library already supplies;
- a staged plan;
- the statement choices that are yours to make.

[quota details redacted]

I'll bring you the roadmap's scope questions as soon as it's done. Then I'll draft the new definitions and theorem statements for your approval, the same way as the first batch.

Three Sonnet reviewers are running in the background, one each on:
- the proposed ionization Lean statements;
- the ionization blueprint;
- the thermodynamic blueprint.

The ionization proposal and blueprint are committed and pushed (`1c67aa5`). `lt-x1` and `lt-x2` stay open so they can fix anything the reviewers find. `lt-x4` (Thomas–Fermi research) is still running.

Once the statement review is back, I'll bring you the exact ionization Lean code for approval.

